# PHASE 1 — COMPREHENSIVE RESEARCH & LITERATURE REVIEW

## ROLE

Act as a multidisciplinary **academic research team** consisting of:

1. A Master's-level academic researcher in Data Science
2. An organizational-design researcher
3. A data/AI engineering researcher
4. An enterprise data strategy consultant
5. A systematic literature-review researcher
6. A research fact-checker and source verifier

Your job is to conduct a **deep, independent, evidence-based literature review** for a Master's course in Enterprise Data Science.

Do not behave like a presentation generator.

Do not simply summarize the assigned book.

Do not limit the research to the sources explicitly mentioned by the professor.

Your objective is to discover and synthesize the **broader body of knowledge** surrounding the topic.

---

# 1. STUDENT AND COURSE CONTEXT

I am a Master's student in **Data Science at Berliner Hochschule für Technik (BHT Berlin)**.

The course is:

> **Enterprise Data Science**

Our group assignment is a **Flipped Classroom literature study**.

The assigned topic is:

> **How to set up a data team, how to grow it, and good examples of successful data teams.**

There are three students in our group.

The eventual classroom session will be approximately:

> **90 minutes total — 30 minutes per student**

However:

## THIS IS PHASE 1 ONLY.

Do NOT create the presentation yet.

Do NOT divide the material among the three students yet.

Do NOT create slides yet.

Do NOT optimize the research for presentation length.

First create the **complete research foundation** from which the presentation can later be developed.

---

# 2. PROFESSOR-PROVIDED REFERENCES

The professor has provided the following sources.

## Assigned Book

**Data Teams: How to Build and Manage Successful Data-Focused Teams**

Reference:

https://www.amazon.de/Data-Teams-Management-Successful-Data-Focused/dp/1484262271

First verify:

- exact title
- exact authors
- publication year
- edition
- publisher
- ISBN
- official publisher page if available
- author information if available

Do not treat Amazon as the academic source.

Use authoritative bibliographic sources wherever possible.

---

## Assigned Article

**Erik Bernhardsson — "The Data Team: A Short Story"**

https://erikbern.com/2021/07/07/the-data-team-a-short-story.html

Treat this as **high-quality practitioner literature**, not peer-reviewed academic evidence.

---

# 3. MOST IMPORTANT INSTRUCTION

## THE ASSIGNED BOOK IS NOT THE BOUNDARY OF THE RESEARCH.

The professor has given the book as a starting point.

The actual research topic is much broader:

> **How organizations build, structure, operate, measure, scale, and evolve effective data teams and data organizations.**

Therefore:

### DO NOT

structure the research as:

> Chapter 1 of the book → Chapter 2 → Chapter 3 → Book summary.

### INSTEAD

use the book as one foundational source and independently research the wider academic and practitioner literature.

Follow:

- citations
- references
- related concepts
- competing theories
- influential authors
- newer research
- empirical studies
- industry evidence
- company case studies
- criticisms
- alternative organizational models

The final research should go **substantially beyond the assigned book**.

---

# 4. CENTRAL RESEARCH QUESTION

Build the research around this central question:

> **How should organizations design, establish, structure, operate, measure, scale, and continuously evolve data teams so that they create sustainable business value while maintaining technical quality, data quality, governance, organizational alignment, and healthy collaboration?**

---

# 5. SUB-RESEARCH QUESTIONS

Investigate all of the following.

## RQ1 — What is a Data Team?

Define "data team" carefully.

Distinguish between:

- BI team
- reporting team
- analytics team
- data science team
- data engineering team
- machine learning team
- data platform team
- analytics engineering team
- data product team
- AI team
- broader data organization

Investigate how the concept has evolved.

Do not assume that all organizations define a data team in the same way.

---

# 6. WHY DO ORGANIZATIONS CREATE DATA TEAMS?

Research:

- business decision-making
- analytics
- automation
- forecasting
- experimentation
- machine learning
- operational efficiency
- customer intelligence
- risk management
- data products
- AI
- competitive advantage

Investigate whether academic research actually demonstrates measurable business value.

Distinguish:

> perceived value

from:

> demonstrated value.

