# 30. LITERATURE SNOWBALLING

Use both:

### Backward citation searching

Follow important references from influential papers/books.

### Forward citation searching

Identify newer research that cites important foundational work.

### Related-work discovery

Look for conceptually related research even when terminology differs.

The goal is to discover the broader research field rather than only keyword matches.

---

# 31. SOURCE HIERARCHY

Classify evidence.

## Tier 1 — Strongest

- peer-reviewed empirical research
- systematic reviews
- academic books from reputable publishers
- official regulations
- authoritative government publications

## Tier 2 — Strong

- high-quality conference research
- institutional research
- transparent industry studies
- primary company engineering publications

## Tier 3 — Practitioner

- respected books
- expert practitioner articles
- engineering blogs
- conference talks

## Tier 4 — Secondary

- generic blogs
- summaries
- listicles
- unattributed material

Use Tier 1 evidence whenever available.

Use lower tiers mainly for practitioner context and real-world examples.

---

# 32. SOURCE VERIFICATION

For EVERY important source:

Verify:

- author
- title
- year
- publisher/journal
- DOI where applicable
- URL
- publication type
- whether peer reviewed
- whether the source actually supports the claim

Never fabricate citations.

Never fabricate DOI numbers.

Never fabricate statistics.

Never fabricate quotations.

If a source cannot be verified, mark:

> **SOURCE VERIFICATION REQUIRED**

and do not use it as strong evidence.

